{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>The saying of him who hears the words of El, and sees the vision of Shaddai — he sees.</b> For every blind person is called in the language of our Rabbis of blessed memory 'sagi nahor' [abundant of light], because the inner vision of the heart and ordinary sight of the eyes contradict one another, and when one of them grows strong, the other weakens. Therefore the inner intellectual vision is great among the blind, for the physical sight does not distract them. And so it is the practice to close one's eyes at the time of reciting 'שְׁמַע יִשְׂרָאֵל' etc., in order to strengthen the intention of the heart, so that one's eyes are below in degree and one's heart is above in degree. Therefore after his eye was dimmed he called himself 'who sees the vision of Shaddai — he sees.' And he said 'falling' (נֹפֵל), because he is blind in his actual eyes, which causes him to fall — but he is open-eyed in the intellectual vision. And he called himself 'hearing the words of El,' for the sense of sight also contradicts the sense of hearing, for a person cannot employ these two senses as one. And as we explained regarding the verse 'הִנֵּה אָנֹכִי בָּא אֵלֶיךָ בְּעַב הֶעָנָן' (Exodus 19:9) — meaning that you will not be able to see the Shekhinah, and He gave the reason 'in order that the people may hear' (בַּעֲבוּר יִשְׁמַע הָעָם), for through this the sense of hearing will be strengthened, as explained in my work עוֹלְלוֹת אֶפְרַיִם, discourse 15 — see there." "And he said: <b>How goodly are your tents, O Jacob.</b> He saw that their openings were not aligned one opposite the other, so that no one could look into his fellow's tent and do harm with the evil eye. One may ask: how is it possible that they should suffer harm from the evil eye, when they themselves are careful of this very thing? And on the contrary — 'like streams they stretch out' (כִּנְחָלִים נִטָּיוּ): he means to say that the tents of Jacob and his dwellings were compared to streams, which cover the fish so that the evil eye should not prevail over them. So too the tents in which those who sit and engage in Torah dwell save from the evil eye, for the evil eye does not prevail over those who sit in tents, who are not seen at the head of all the streets. And nevertheless their Torah proclaims concerning them: in the streets she gives forth her voice (בָּרְחוֹבוֹת תִּתֵּן קוֹלָהּ), and her springs spread outward (וְיָפֻצוּ מַעְיְנוֹתָיו חוּצָה), in the broad places, streams of water (בָּרְחֹבוֹת פַּלְגֵי מָיִם). Therefore it is said 'like streams they stretch out, like gardens by the river, like aloes HaShem planted' (כִּנְחָלִים נִטָּיוּ כַּאֲהָלִים נָטַע ה׳), for the tents and the streams have one law between them."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:08.914670620Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}